ed":"2026-09-16T17:33:06.391573Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-16T17:33:07.090036Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport os\n\ndef predict_full_volume_batched(model, full_volume, patch_size=(1,32,64,64), batch_size=16, tmp_dir=\"/kaggle/working/_pred_tmp\"):\n    \"\"\"\n    Changes from the original:\n    - stride == patch_size (no overlap), instead of 50% overlap in every\n      spatial dim. That was causing 2x2x2 = 8x more patches (and 8x more CPU\n      forward passes) than necessary -- the main reason this was slow.\n    - output/div_output are memory-mapped to disk (np.memmap) instead of\n      allocated as in-RAM float32 arrays. The old version held 3 full-volume\n      arrays (output, div_output, counts) in RAM simultaneously before doing\n      any work; for a large volume that alone can OOM the kernel. memmap\n      behaves like a normal numpy array for slicing/writing, but the OS pages\n      it to/from disk instead of requiring it all in RAM at once.\n    - counts is gone entirely since there's no overlap to average over.\n    - the last tile in each dimension is clamped to the volume edge so the\n      remainder is still covered, instead of being silently skipped when the\n      volume doesn't divide evenly by patch_size (a pre-existing coverage gap).\n\n    Trade-off: with no overlap, predictions can show faint seams at patch\n    boundaries (no blending between adjacent tiles). If that hurts your\n    watershed segmentation quality, the fix is to blend only a few voxels at\n    each boundary rather than reintroducing full 50% overlap -- worth\n    revisiting once this fits in memory and runs in reasonable time.\n    \"\"\"\n    pt, pz, py, px = patch_size\n    t_max, z_max, y_max, x_max = full_volume.shape\n\n    os.makedirs(tmp_dir, exist_ok=True)\n    out_path = os.path.join(tmp_dir, \"center_pred.dat\")\n    div_path = os.path.join(tmp_dir, \"div_pred.dat\")\n    output = 